      title: '+ Develop a systematic diagnostic framework for failed back surgery syndrome. How do you differentiate the multiple potential pain generators?',
      content: '**Failed Back Surgery Syndrome (FBSS) — Diagnostic Framework**:\nFBSS is not a single diagnosis but a clinical scenario with multiple potential etiologies requiring systematic evaluation.\n\n**Structural/Surgical Causes**:\n1. **Pseudoarthrosis (failed fusion)**: Incidence 5-35% after lumbar fusion. Diagnosed with dynamic flexion-extension radiographs showing motion at fused segment, or CT with fine cuts showing lucency around hardware. May require revision surgery if clinically significant.\n2. **Adjacent segment disease**: Accelerated degeneration above or below fusion (occurs in 30% within 10 years). This patient\'s L2-3 stenosis above fusion is suspicious. Diagnostic selective nerve root block at L2-3 can confirm.\n3. **Hardware failure**: Screw loosening, rod fracture, cage migration. Diagnosed with CT reconstruction; plain films may miss subtle failure.\n4. **Recurrent disc herniation**: At operated or adjacent levels. MRI with gadolinium differentiates recurrent disc (no enhancement) from epidural fibrosis (enhances on contrast).\n5. **Epidural fibrosis/arachnoiditis**: Post-surgical scar tissue encasing nerve roots. MRI shows clumping of nerve roots in the thecal sac (arachnoiditis) or epidural scar tissue. No surgical treatment for arachnoiditis.\n6. **Foraminal stenosis**: Hardware or bony overgrowth compressing exiting nerve roots. May be missed on standard MRI; CT myelogram may be needed.\n7. **Sacroiliac joint dysfunction**: Altered biomechanics after fusion increase SI joint stress. Diagnosed with combination of 3 or more provocative physical exam tests (FABER, Gaenslen, thigh thrust, compression, distraction) and diagnostic SI joint injection (>75% pain relief).\n\n**Non-Structural Causes**:\n1. **Central sensitization/nociplastic pain**: As evidenced by allodynia in remote areas, temporal summation, and CSI score. This represents altered central pain processing and will not respond to further surgical intervention.\n2. **Myofascial pain**: Paraspinal deconditioning and trigger points from chronic guarding.\n3. **Opioid-induced hyperalgesia**: Paradoxical increased pain sensitivity from chronic high-dose opioids. Differentiated from tolerance by pain that is diffuse, extends beyond original distribution, and worsens with dose escalation.\n\n**Diagnostic Workup**:\n- CT lumbar spine with fine cuts through hardware — assess fusion status and hardware integrity\n- MRI lumbar spine with and without gadolinium — differentiate disc from fibrosis, evaluate adjacent segments\n- Flexion-extension radiographs — assess stability at fused segments\n- EMG/NCS bilateral lower extremities — characterize radiculopathy vs. peripheral neuropathy\n- Diagnostic selective nerve root blocks at L2-3 if adjacent segment disease suspected\n- Diagnostic SI joint injections bilaterally (image-guided)\n- Laboratory: ESR, CRP (rule out infection), CBC, metabolic panel, vitamin D, testosterone (opioid endocrinopathy)\n- PDMP query, urine drug screen with confirmatory testing\n\n**Key Principle**: Multiple pain generators typically coexist in FBSS. Treatment must address all contributors simultaneously, not seek a single "source."',
