ality Inventory-2), stable on medications, no active substance use disorder\n  - **PROCESS trial**: Level 1 evidence showing SCS superior to conventional medical management for FBSS\n- **Intrathecal drug delivery system (IDDS)**: Consider if SCS trial fails. Allows targeted delivery of opioid (morphine or hydromorphone) and/or ziconotide at a fraction of systemic doses. Requires successful intrathecal trial and psychological clearance. Risk of granuloma formation at catheter tip with chronic intrathecal opioids.\n\n**Phase 4 — Intensive Interdisciplinary Pain Rehabilitation (Concurrent)**:\n- Enroll in 3-4 week intensive program (4-6 hours daily)\n- **Physical therapy**: Aquatic therapy progressing to land-based. Quota-based graded activity. Core stabilization, cardiovascular reconditioning. Goal: functional restoration independent of pain level.\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy**: Pain catastrophizing reduction, activity pacing, sleep hygiene. Address fear-avoidance and kinesiophobia (Tampa Scale for Kinesiophobia).\n- **Pain neuroscience education**: Explain central sensitization, neural plasticity, and the biopsychosocial model\n- **Vocational counseling**: Even if full return to electrician work is not possible, explore alternative employment or volunteer activity for occupational identity.\n\n**Medication Optimization**:\n- Duloxetine 60 mg daily (initiated in Phase 1)\n- Gabapentin dose optimization: ensure at adequate dose (up to 1200 mg TID) or consider switch to pregabalin for improved bioavailability\n- Low-dose naltrexone (4.5 mg nightly): emerging evidence for central sensitization through glial cell modulation\n- Gradual opioid taper (10% every 2-4 weeks) after stabilization. Target: lowest effective dose or complete discontinuation\n- Consider buprenorphine transition if opioid use disorder identified or if OIH confirmed\n- Topical agents: lidocaine patches to axial spine, capsaicin 8% patch to areas of allodynia',
