      content: '**Social Security Disability (SSDI) Process**:\n- SSDI uses a 5-step sequential evaluation: (1) Is the applicant working at substantial gainful activity level? (2) Is the impairment severe? (3) Does it meet or equal a listed impairment? (4) Can they do past relevant work? (5) Can they do any other work?\n- **Listing 1.04 (Disorders of the Spine)**: Requires evidence of nerve root compression, spinal arachnoiditis, or lumbar stenosis resulting in pseudoclaudication with inability to ambulate effectively\n- The physician provides objective medical evidence; the adjudicator makes the disability determination\n\n**Responding to the Disability Letter Request**:\n- Provide an objective, factual medical report documenting: diagnoses, surgical history, exam findings, functional limitations, treatment history, and prognosis\n- Use standardized outcome measures (Oswestry Disability Index, 6-Minute Walk Test results) to support functional limitations\n- **Do not** state the patient is "totally and permanently disabled" — this is a legal/administrative determination, not a medical one\n- **Do not** opine on surgical negligence — this is outside your role as a treating physician and creates a conflict of interest\n\n**Responding to the Attorney**:\n- Release records only with signed patient authorization (HIPAA)\n- Decline to provide opinions on surgical negligence. State clearly: "As the treating physician, it is not appropriate for me to render opinions on the standard of care provided by other physicians. I am happy to provide objective documentation of the patient\'s current condition and functional status."\n- If asked to testify, this would require a separate agreement as an expert witness, not as a treating physician\n\n**Disability vs. Functional Restoration — The Tension**:\n- Studies show that patients who receive disability determination often have worse pain outcomes than those who pursue functional restoration\n- However, some patients with severe FB